// @run
// @log 1
// @log 101
// A nested `<T>` generic CALLED from its own frame, sharing its name with a top-level
// CONCRETE function. This used to be INVALID WASM at `vl check` rc 0 (#1263).
//
// Two resolvers read this call site. `wasmEmit`'s by-name call path lowers `id(1)` through
// `fnIndexOfInScopeChain` — the language's innermost-scope rule — so it targets the NESTED
// `id`. The monomorphizer's call-site accounting used the FLAT `fnIndexOf`, which returns
// the FIRST `fnStmts` slot carrying the name: the top-level concrete `id`. Seeing a
// non-generic callee it instantiated nothing, so the nested `<T>` template stayed
// un-instantiated and the prune replaced it with a no-op `() -> void` stub — while the
// emitter went on calling it with `(env, i32)`. "type mismatch: expected structref, found
// i32", written to disk at build rc 0 on the pre-fix compiler.
//
// The fix points the monomorphizer at `fnIndexOfInScope`, `emit_classify`'s twin of that
// same chain walk, so both resolvers now answer with the innermost declaration.
//
// The twin that must NOT move is `exported-concrete-nested-generic-homonym.vl`, where the
// nested generic is UNCALLED and correctly keeps taking the silent no-op stub.
function id(n: i32): i32 { return n + 100 }

function host(): i32 {
  function id<T>(x: T): T { return x }
  return id(1)
}

print(host())
print(id(1))
